   How terminal browsers render the web                              Contents
                                                                     Text first
   By A. Writer · 8 Oct 2026 · 6 min read · Share                    Numbers
                                                                     Code
   Rendering a web page into a grid of characters is an
   exercise in compromise. A cell is eight pixels wide and
   sixteen tall, a paragraph in a proportional font rarely
   lines up with it, and yet the result can be remarkably            Related
   readable when the renderer respects the structure of the          Why pixels lie
   page rather than its pixels.                                      Fast diffs
                                                                   ▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄
   Text first

   The first rule is that text should stay text. When a
   paragraph is drawn as coloured blocks it turns into noise;
   when it is drawn as characters it stays crisp and
   selectable, even if its position is only approximate. Links
   keep their underline and colour, headings keep their
   weight.

   ▖  Structure survives quantisation; pixels do not.
